  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-237526",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $3$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fourths*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/606a6bfa9efc29dab451ce936003a0668664fdd0e853e98264ecc0a0af062fd9.png)\n\n \n\n- The fourths are grouped, and each group contains $\\frac{2}{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/f7747d516865a13babe44cbf6d2ceab399b1106ae0f8eedc3b849967d436d7da.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $6$ groups in total.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/cb4655782705d05f32db002c8e7ace17c672e6e521650d351bcc81461a10c5f3.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $3$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{2}{4},$ we get $6$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\,3\\,}÷\\frac{2}{4}=\\boxed{\\,6\\,}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $3$ and $6.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
